Build Specification — App 36: The Error Taxonomy
Completeness status: COMPLETE. All sections delivered in one pass. No truncation. Formatted for copy-paste. Long lists are broken into short lines.

1. What It Is
A structured record of every error a student makes, what kind of error it is, and what it reveals about their understanding. An error is a mistake in a problem, an answer, a proof, a calculation, a decision, or an argument. The Error Taxonomy records each error with its type, its description, the underlying misconception, the recommended intervention, and the provenance.

It reuses:

the STAG Schema Library (App 17)

the Provenance-Aware Annotation Database (App 20)

the Revision History Viewer (App 7)

the Misconception Database (App 31)

the Method Tracker (App 32)

the Learning Provenance System (App 33)

the Autofill Library Service (App 49)

It is for teachers, tutors, education researchers, and anyone who wants to understand why students make mistakes.

2. Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Accept deposits of errors from users.

Store each error with its subject, its topic, its description, and its provenance.

Record the error type: conceptual, procedural, factual, computational, logical, strategic, careless, or other.

Record the underlying misconception, if any, linked to the Misconception Database.

Record the method that was being used, if any, linked to the Method Tracker.

Record the recommended intervention.

Record the student's response to the intervention, if known.

Record the context in which the error was made: the problem, the question, the setting.

Record the frequency: how often the student makes this error.

Record the persistence: how hard the error is to correct.

Enforce provenance completeness.

Enforce versioning.

Enforce supersession acyclicity.

Support querying by error ID, student, subject, topic, error type, date, and keywords.

Support full-text search of the description, the recommended intervention, and the notes.

Support aggregation queries: count by subject, by error type, by student, by year, by frequency, by persistence.

Support exporting an error as JSON, PROV-O, and Turtle.

Support importing an error from JSON.

Support bulk import and bulk export.

Support a REST API for CRUD operations.

Support a GraphQL API for flexible queries.

Support a command-line interface for administration.

Support authentication and authorization.

Support audit logging.

Support backup and restore.

Support a web interface.

Support moderation.

Support linking an error to a Misconception Database record.

Support linking an error to a Method Tracker record.

Support linking an error to a Learning Provenance System record.

Support the error map: a visualisation of errors by subject and by type.

Support the error frequency curve: a visualisation of how often a student makes an error over time.

Support the intervention comparison view: a visualisation that compares two or more interventions for the same error.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic.

Fast: query a typical error in under 100 milliseconds.

Scalable: support up to 10^6 errors.

Versioned: the taxonomy records the STAG schema version, the error schema version, and the taxonomy version.

Reproducible.

Portable.

Well-documented.

